# "the word is nonempty and both its first and its last symbol are a",
# written with leftmost attention -- the head shape learned programs reach
# for (see the uhat sweep), and the reason `BraspNormalize` exists.
#
# The leftmost score reads the *query* position as well as the witness, so
# translating it needs the case split over `@i` variables, not the trivial
# one-residual path: under `is_a@i` the witness is the first symbol position,
# under `is_b@i` it is the first `b` (whose value is then never `a`).
#
# Checked against `spec__first_and_last_a.brasp`, which spells out the same
# language with rightmost attention only.
alphabet a b

is_bos = bos
is_a = symbol a
is_b = symbol b
seen_symbol = rightmost(!is_bos@j, true)
at_first = !is_bos & !seen_symbol
h = leftmost(is_a@i & !is_bos@j | is_b@i & is_b@j, is_a@j)
accept = h | at_first & is_a

output accept
